{
	"nodes": [
		{
			"id": "header",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## Pharmacology\n\nDomain hub: drug exposure, target action, adaptation, safety principles, drug classes and how treatment evidence is measured.\n\n[[Pharmacology Map|Open the domain map]] - snapshot of 2026-09-27; the map's *In this folder* section is the live view.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 3040,
			"height": 170,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Pharmacokinetics and ADME]]**\n*process* - 6 sources\n\nAbsorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, each linked to the clinical consequences it explains.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Half-life and steady state]]**\n*process* - 5 sources\n\nHalf-life, accumulation and steady state as exposure models, with lithium as the worked example of why timing and clearance matter.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 598,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Pharmacogenomics]]**\n*mechanism* - 8 sources\n\nHow inherited variation in drug-metabolising genes such as CYP2D6 and CYP2C19 and in HLA genes changes drug exposure and the risk of severe reactions, what testing has achieved in trials, and what it cannot predict for an individual.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 860,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "g0",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Exposure",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 840
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Pharmacodynamics and receptors]]**\n*mechanism* - 6 sources\n\nDrug targets, agonism and antagonism, the gap between occupancy and clinical effect, and receptor-level adaptation.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Target binding and dose-response]]**\n*mechanism* - 7 sources\n\nAffinity, efficacy and potency: what binding measurements establish and what they leave open.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 598,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Receptor adaptation tolerance and dependence]]**\n*process* - 6 sources\n\nHow repeated drug action changes the system, and the difference between tolerance, dependence and withdrawal.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 836,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "g1",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Action",
			"x": 460,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 768
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Drug classes and mechanisms overview]]**\n*framework* - 10 sources\n\nHow psychotropic drug classes are organised by target and mechanism, and what the class labels do and do not tell you.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Antidepressant mechanisms]]**\n*mechanism* - 7 sources\n\nWhat antidepressants do at the synapse, why clinical change is slower than the pharmacology, and what that implies.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 598,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Antipsychotic mechanisms]]**\n*mechanism* - 9 sources\n\nD2 blockade as the time-scoped shared mechanism of the dopamine-blocking class, the pathways behind response, movement and endocrine effects, and the 2024 muscarinic alternative.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 836,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Stimulant and non-stimulant mechanisms]]**\n*mechanism* - 7 sources\n\nHow ADHD medications increase catecholamine signalling by different routes, and how their evidence is measured.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 1122,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_4",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors]]**\n*framework* - 16 sources\n\nThe SSRI class: shared transporter mechanism, uses at population level, class-level harms and interactions, and how the evidence differs in PTSD, bipolar depression and autism.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 1384,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_5",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors]]**\n*framework* - 16 sources\n\nThe SNRI class: dual transporter block, use in depression, anxiety and chronic pain, class-level harms and interactions, and where the evidence differs in bipolar depression, ADHD and PTSD.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 1694,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_6",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Tricyclic antidepressants]]**\n*framework* - 13 sources\n\nThe tricyclic class: dual reuptake block with anticholinergic burden, second-line use at population level, overdose risk, and how the evidence differs in ADHD, autism, PTSD and bipolar depression.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 2004,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_7",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Monoamine oxidase inhibitors]]**\n*framework* - 7 sources\n\nThe MAOI class: enzyme inhibition rather than transporter block, selectivity and reversibility, third-line use at population level, food and drug interactions, toxicity, and the gaps in bipolar and other condition evidence.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 2290,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_8",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Atypical antidepressants]]**\n*framework* - 13 sources\n\nA residual group - bupropion, mirtazapine, trazodone, vortioxetine, agomelatine and similar drugs - defined by what they are not; their separate receptor profiles, population-level uses, harms, and how the evidence differs in ADHD, bipolar depression and PTSD.",
			"x": 1340,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 274,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_9",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Ketamine and rapid-acting antidepressants]]**\n*framework* - 6 sources\n\nKetamine, esketamine and newer rapid-acting approaches: glutamatergic and downstream plasticity mechanisms, what the 24-hour evidence shows, what it leaves unknown, and how thin the bipolar evidence is.",
			"x": 1340,
			"y": 694,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_10",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Psychedelics and MDMA-assisted therapy]]**\n*framework* - 6 sources\n\nPsilocybin for depression and MDMA-assisted therapy for PTSD: serotonergic starting points, what the trials show under imperfect blinding, the safety gaps, and the US regulatory history to the 2024 decision.",
			"x": 1340,
			"y": 1004,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 274,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_11",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[First-generation antipsychotics]]**\n*framework* - 10 sources\n\nThe older dopamine-blocking antipsychotics: members, D2 mechanism, why potency matters more than generation, population-level uses, movement and endocrine harms, and why the generation label is contested.",
			"x": 1340,
			"y": 1338,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_12",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Second-generation antipsychotics]]**\n*framework* - 10 sources\n\nThe newer antipsychotics, including the D2/D3 partial agonists aripiprazole, brexpiprazole and cariprazine: two ways of acting at one receptor, phase-specific uses in schizophrenia and bipolar disorder, and metabolic, movement and partial-agonist harms.",
			"x": 1340,
			"y": 1624,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 274,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_13",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Muscarinic antipsychotics]]**\n*framework* - 9 sources\n\nAntipsychotics that act on muscarinic acetylcholine receptors instead of blocking D2: xanomeline-trospium and the M4 modulator emraclidine, their evidence, their cholinergic side-effect profile and what remains unknown.",
			"x": 1340,
			"y": 1958,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_14",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Anticonvulsant mood stabilisers]]**\n*framework* - 11 sources\n\nValproate, carbamazepine and lamotrigine in bipolar disorder: a group defined by use rather than mechanism, their different phase and polarity profiles, pregnancy and skin-reaction risks, and lithium as the comparator.",
			"x": 1340,
			"y": 2268,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_15",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Antiseizure medications]]**\n*framework* - 12 sources\n\nDrugs that suppress seizures by damping neuronal excitability: their mechanism families, their uses in epilepsy and in bipolar disorder, anxiety and pain, and the class-level risks in pregnancy, skin, mood and behaviour.",
			"x": 1340,
			"y": 2578,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_16",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Benzodiazepines and Z-drugs]]**\n*framework* - 11 sources\n\nGABA-A receptor modulators used for insomnia, anxiety and seizures: how they work, what trials show over weeks, and the dependence, falls, complex sleep behaviours, opioid co-use and trauma-related harms that accumulate with use.",
			"x": 1730,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_17",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Hypnotics and sleep medicines]]**\n*framework* - 16 sources\n\nThe main routes by which medicines promote sleep - GABA-A modulation, orexin blockade, melatonin signalling and histamine blockade - what each is used for, and how they differ from benzodiazepines in evidence, tolerability and dependence.",
			"x": 1730,
			"y": 670,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_18",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Adrenergic blockers in psychiatry]]**\n*framework* - 14 sources\n\nPrazosin, propranolol, clonidine and guanfacine: drugs acting on noradrenaline receptors, their evidence for trauma-related nightmares, anxiety and ADHD, and why blocking alpha-1 or beta receptors differs from stimulating alpha-2 receptors.",
			"x": 1730,
			"y": 980,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "g2",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Classes",
			"x": 920,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 1200,
			"height": 2558
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Therapeutic index, monitoring and interactions]]**\n*framework* - 3 sources\n\nTherapeutic index, monitoring logic and interaction classes, with the explicit boundary that this is reference material rather than personal guidance.",
			"x": 2190,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Efficacy versus tolerability]]**\n*framework* - 4 sources\n\nEfficacy and tolerability as separate endpoints, and how rater and comparator choices change what an effect estimate means.",
			"x": 2190,
			"y": 646,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Trial endpoints, benefit and harms]]**\n*framework* - 8 sources\n\nHow outcomes are selected and reported in treatment trials, and why the choice of endpoint changes what a result means.",
			"x": 2190,
			"y": 884,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#F07A3C"
		},
		{
			"id": "g3",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Safety and evidence",
			"x": 2160,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 792
		},
